# dtmf-inbound-abcd.hex — four inbound DTMF digits, 'A' 'B' 'C' 'D'.
#
# Hand-built octet by octet from RFC 5456 (provenance rule 1,
# Tests/FIXTURES.md). Direction: peer (source call number 0x0042) → us
# (0x0001), on a call that is up.
#
# The A–D digits exist in DTMF and are in the §8.2 subclass domain
# ("0-9, A-D, *, #") even though a telephone keypad does not show them, so
# they are the interesting inbound case. Their ASCII codes are 0x41–0x44.
#
# Each datagram is a 12-octet Full Frame header and nothing else (§8.1.1,
# §8.2.1); the Data field is "Undefined" for DTMF, so there is no payload:
#
#   80 42        F = 1 + source call number 0x0042                     §8.1.1
#   00 01        R = 0 + destination call number 0x0001                §8.1.1
#   00 00 xx xx  the peer's own 32-bit call clock: 1000, 1200, 1400
#                and 1600 ms                                           §8.1.1
#   02..05       OSeqno, incrementing once per reliable Full Frame     §7
#   01           ISeqno = 1 throughout                                 §8.1.1
#   01           Frame Type 0x01 = DTMF                          §8.2, §8.2.1
#   41..44       C = 0, subclass field = ASCII 'A'…'D'          §8.1.1, §8.2.1
#
# Each of the four is a complete digit on its own: RFC 5456 has no DTMF
# BEGIN, no END, no duration and no volume (notes §14), so a receiver that
# waits for a second frame to close a digit waits for ever.
#
8042 0001 000003e8 02 01 01 41
8042 0001 000004b0 03 01 01 42
8042 0001 00000578 04 01 01 43
8042 0001 00000640 05 01 01 44
